The following component is the author's own work, used under the MIT Licence:

  BigBubbleMuff (haiku/source/dsp)
  Copyright (c) 2026 BigBubbleMuff contributors

  Vendored as deps/bbm/Oversampler.h, Filters.h and AudioMath.h. Oversampler.h
  is the 4x half-band polyphase-IIR oversampler the Boost pedal's nonlinear
  solver runs inside; its coefficients were solved by a minimax filter designer
  and its measured figures are recorded beside each table in the file.
  Filters.h is a TPT one-pole section; AudioMath.h supplies a linear parameter
  smoother and a NaN guard.

  A note on why the per-file grant governs. That project's top-level COPYING is
  GNU GPL version 3, because its Linux build links JUCE. Its haiku/ subtree,
  from which these three files come, carries its own MIT LICENSE, and every file
  in it declares SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT in its own header. That subtree is
  framework-free by construction -- it is described in its own comments as "the
  Linux engine with the framework taken out" -- and includes nothing GPL, so the
  GPLv3 at the repository root is the licence of the JUCE-linked aggregate, not
  of these files. The author of Rations is also the rights-holder of that work
  and has confirmed this reading. This is the same relicensing-of-own-work
  situation already recorded below for the art scripts.

  Not taken from that project: DiodeClipper.h, TransistorStage.h, Netlist.h and
  ToneStack.h, all of which encode Big Muff Pi component values and topology.
  The Boost pedal's clipper is a different circuit and is written against the
  Ibanez TS-9 analysis in David Yeh's Stanford CCRMA thesis (see the reference
  set in third_party/refs/pedals/SOURCES.md), citing that thesis' own equation
  numbers. Where it follows DiodeClipper.h's numerical technique -- the asinh
  initial guess and the overflow-bounded exponential that keep a Newton solve
  real-time-safe -- it says so at the site.

